{
 "indicator": {
  "definition": {
   "definition": "The Statutory Liquidity Ratio is the minimum share of a bank's net demand and time liabilities it must hold in approved liquid assets — cash, gold, and unencumbered government and other approved securities, including Treasury Bills of the Government of India. It is governed by the same Master Direction as the CRR (issued under Section 35A of the Banking Regulation Act, 1949 and pursuant to Section 42 of the RBI Act, 1934) and published in the WSS 'Ratios and Rates' table. A reduction frees banks to lend; it also lowers the demand for government securities, so an SLR change is watched on both the credit and the bond-market side.",
   "source_name": "RBI Master Direction — Cash Reserve Ratio and Statutory Liquidity Ratio Directions, 2021",
   "source_url": "https://www.rbi.org.in/Scripts/BS_ViewMasDirections.aspx?id=12131",
   "term": "Statutory Liquidity Ratio (SLR)"
  },
